			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825833">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>then it was going to have an initial experiment log that I was going to use the experiment log format style I found.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>I am not convinced a test log would work with cats seeing specters. Why not just a summary of findings in the Description? Honestly, although the origin of the site has deep ties to test logs&#8230; I personally rarely find them interesting&#8230; And think a simple paragraph explaining what research has revealed to flow better.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>in my draft I have it that the cat with experience a piloerection in its tail and begin to growl while following the entity with its eyes and this would alert the MTF to its presents.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>I guess you might relate them to how Ghost hunter shows misuse EMF readers too find ghosts but these would actually work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Well, this doesn't really answer my question. Because an EMF reader is a machine that you can hold. A cat is <strong>not</strong>. So how does an MTF utilize a cat in the field? Let them loose in the area and follow them around? Keep them in cages? Good luck getting a cat to go where you want them to is my point.</em></p>
<hr />
<p>So while I am fairly sure of the plot of your article: <em>Researcher discovers cats can see ghosts -&gt; confirms with testing -&gt; proposes they be used with MTFs -&gt; and we follow their use in the field;</em> That plot really doesn't tell me much about the narrative. Is there a thematic arc to this? Where does the reader start, and what is the journey they go on?</p>
<p>Moreover, I would recommend against the note at the end trope for your first article. Its overused, Lots of us are guilty of it&#8230; but I feel like you're going to get some heat for trying it in the end of the first article you use. Also, it just isn't usually satisfying without a big reveal. This, as proposed, is more of the &quot;next logical step.&quot; Probably more interesting to give us a log of the cats being used in the field, make that interesting and just end with &quot;Further use of SCP-XXXX to assist in containing spectral anomalies is under consideration.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

